# Copy this file to .env.local (git-ignored) and fill in your Supabase project's
# values - Settings -> API Keys in the dashboard. Both are required at build/dev
# time; src/supabase/client.ts throws a clear error on startup if either is missing.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY is the publishable key (format `sb_publishable_...`;
# Supabase's current replacement for the legacy JWT "anon" key - a legacy anon key
# still works here too, since createClient() accepts either). It is low-privilege
# and safe to ship in the client bundle: see
# docs/adr/0001-host-epoch-and-rpc-only-access.md for why the schema is designed so
# that key alone can't list or tamper with Rooms. Never put a secret key
# (`sb_secret_...`, ex-"service_role") in a Vite env var - anything prefixed
# VITE_ is bundled into the client and shipped to every browser.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_your-key-here

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TURN relay (optional to run the app, REQUIRED for it to work for everyone)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# With neither block below set, the app falls back to STUN alone and logs a
# warning. STUN only reports the address a NAT showed the STUN server; a
# symmetric NAT (most mobile data, via carrier-grade NAT) hands out a different
# external port per destination, so that address is not where the other side
# must send. A host behind one is unreachable by EVERY guest, from EVERY
# network, until a relay exists. See
# docs/rca/0001-guests-cannot-connect-across-networks.md
#
# Production: an endpoint that mints SHORT-LIVED credentials per request.
# A deployable one, backed by Cloudflare Realtime's TURN service, is in
# supabase/functions/turn-credentials/ - its header has the deploy steps.
# VITE_TURN_CREDENTIALS_URL=https://your-project-ref.functions.supabase.co/turn-credentials
#
# Local testing only: static credentials, compiled into the bundle. Anyone who
# opens devtools has them and can use your relay, so do not ship these. Include
# a TCP and a turns:-on-443 URL alongside UDP - networks that drop UDP outright
# are the other half of this problem, and only TLS on 443 reliably gets out.
# VITE_TURN_URLS=turn:localhost:3478?transport=udp,turn:localhost:3478?transport=tcp,turns:localhost:5349?transport=tcp
# VITE_TURN_USERNAME=your-turn-user
# VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=your-turn-password
